#lang saffron
;; saffron-lang-plan S5/D4: the compiled dynamic operator set is BOUNDED, and
;; the boundary is reported rather than guessed at.
;;
;; The two back ends get here by opposite routes.  The interpreter resolves a
;; dynamic operator by calling `builtin_lookup` with the TypeKind read off the
;; value's own tag and then running `eval_builtin` -- so it inherits the whole
;; builtin table for free, and `(cons x 0)` with `x : any` just works.  A
;; compiled program carries no builtin table, so the compiled half enumerates
;; its operators, and it enumerates the scalar ones: arithmetic, the bit
;; operators, comparison, `not`, truthiness (`and` / `or` / `if`) and `println`.
;;
;; `cons` is outside that set on purpose -- it builds a linked structure, and
;; containers that can hold dynamic values are S6's subject, not S5's.  So this
;; is the shape of the remaining gap, and the diagnostic names the operator and
;; points at the interpreter, which runs this program today.
;;
;; Guessing would be a miscompile rather than a missing feature: the arguments
;; are `tur_tagged_t` boxes, so emitting the static operator would build a cons
;; cell out of tag words.  That is why S3 introduced a distinct EX_DYN_OP node
;; instead of a flag on EX_BUILTIN -- the emitter cannot mistake one for a
;; statically-resolved operator.
;;
;; requires.compiled: the assertion is precisely that the COMPILED back end
;; refuses.  Under --interpret this program builds the cell and prints 1, so
;; run-turi.sh must not look for the diagnostic here.
(defn dyn [x] x)
(defn main [] : int
  (println (cons (dyn 1) 0))
  0)
